Task 1: Enable Live Migration for VMs using virtctl
Subtask 1.1: Verify OpenShift Virtualization Installation
First, let's confirm that OpenShift Virtualization is properly installed and configured for live migration.

Check OpenShift Virtualization Operator status:
oc get csv -n openshift-cnv | grep kubevirt
Verify HyperConverged resource:
oc get hyperconverged -n openshift-cnv
Check if live migration is enabled:
oc get kubevirt -n openshift-cnv -o yaml | grep -A 10 liveMigration
Subtask 1.2: Install and Configure virtctl
The virtctl command-line tool is essential for managing virtual machines and performing live migrations.

Download and install virtctl:
# Get the latest virtctl version
VIRTCTL_VERSION=$(oc get kubevirt.kubevirt.io/kubevirt -n openshift-cnv -o=jsonpath="{.status.observedKubeVirtVersion}")

# Download virtctl
curl -L -o virtctl https://github.com/kubevirt/kubevirt/releases/download/${VIRTCTL_VERSION}/virtctl-${VIRTCTL_VERSION}-linux-amd64

# Make it executable
chmod +x virtctl

# Move to PATH
sudo mv virtctl /usr/local/bin/
Verify virtctl installation:
virtctl version
Subtask 1.3: Configure Live Migration Settings
Configure the cluster-wide live migration settings for optimal performance.

Create a live migration configuration:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: KubeVirt
metadata:
  name: kubevirt
  namespace: openshift-cnv
spec:
  configuration:
    liveMigrationConfig:
      completionTimeoutPerGiB: 800
      progressTimeout: 150
      parallelMigrationsPerCluster: 5
      parallelOutboundMigrationsPerNode: 2
      bandwidthPerMigration: "64Mi"
      allowAutoConverge: false
      allowPostCopy: false
EOF
Verify the configuration:
oc get kubevirt kubevirt -n openshift-cnv -o yaml | grep -A 15 liveMigrationConfig
Subtask 1.4: Check Node Readiness for Live Migration
Ensure all nodes in the cluster are properly configured for live migration.

List all nodes:
oc get nodes
Check node labels for live migration:
oc get nodes --show-labels | grep kubevirt
Verify shared storage availability:
oc get storageclass
Task 2: Migrate a Running VM Between OpenShift Nodes
Subtask 2.1: Create a Test Virtual Machine
Let's create a virtual machine that we'll use for testing live migration.

Create a VM with shared storage:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: migration-test-vm
  namespace: default
spec:
  running: true
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: migration-test-vm
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 2
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: containerdisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: cloudinitdisk
          interfaces:
          - masquerade: {}
            name: default
        machine:
          type: pc-q35-rhel8.4.0
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 2Gi
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/fedora-cloud-container-disk-demo
        name: containerdisk
      - cloudInitNoCloud:
          userData: |
            #cloud-config
            password: fedora
            chpasswd: { expire: False }
            packages:
              - stress
        name: cloudinitdisk
EOF
Wait for the VM to be ready:
oc wait --for=condition=Ready vmi/migration-test-vm --timeout=300s
Check VM status and current node:
oc get vmi migration-test-vm -o wide
Subtask 2.2: Prepare VM for Migration Testing
Before migrating, let's set up some workload in the VM to test continuity.

Connect to the VM console:
virtctl console migration-test-vm
Inside the VM, start a continuous process (login with user: fedora, password: fedora):
# Create a simple script that writes timestamps
while true; do
  echo "$(date): VM is running on $(hostname)" >> /tmp/migration-test.log
  sleep 5
done &

# Start a light CPU load
stress --cpu 1 --timeout 600s &
Exit the console (Ctrl+] to disconnect)
Subtask 2.3: Perform Live Migration
Now let's perform the actual live migration of our running VM.

Check available target nodes:
oc get nodes -l node-role.kubernetes.io/worker=
Initiate live migration:
virtctl migrate migration-test-vm
Monitor migration progress:
# Watch the migration status
oc get vmim -w

# In another terminal, monitor the VM instance
watch "oc get vmi migration-test-vm -o wide"
Check migration details:
oc describe vmim $(oc get vmim -o name | head -1)
Subtask 2.4: Alternative Migration Methods
Learn different ways to trigger live migration.

Migration using YAML manifest:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachineInstanceMigration
metadata:
  name: manual-migration-$(date +%s)
spec:
  vmiName: migration-test-vm
EOF
Migration to specific node:
# First, get target node name
TARGET_NODE=$(oc get nodes -l node-role.kubernetes.io/worker= -o jsonpath='{.items[1].metadata.name}')

cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachineInstanceMigration
metadata:
  name: targeted-migration-$(date +%s)
spec:
  vmiName: migration-test-vm
  nodeSelector:
    kubernetes.io/hostname: ${TARGET_NODE}
EOF
Task 3: Test Migration Performance and Verify VM State Post-Migration
Subtask 3.1: Monitor Migration Performance
Let's analyze the performance characteristics of our live migration.

Create a performance monitoring script:
cat << 'EOF' > migration-monitor.sh
#!/bin/bash

VM_NAME="migration-test-vm"
MIGRATION_NAME=$(oc get vmim -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}' 2>/dev/null)

if [ -z "$MIGRATION_NAME" ]; then
    echo "No active migration found"
    exit 1
fi

echo "Monitoring migration: $MIGRATION_NAME"
echo "=================================="

while oc get vmim $MIGRATION_NAME >/dev/null 2>&1; do
    # Get migration status
    STATUS=$(oc get vmim $MIGRATION_NAME -o jsonpath='{.status.phase}')
    
    # Get VM current node
    CURRENT_NODE=$(oc get vmi $VM_NAME -o jsonpath='{.status.nodeName}')
    
    # Get migration progress if available
    PROGRESS=$(oc get vmim $MIGRATION_NAME -o jsonpath='{.status}' | jq -r '.conditions[]? | select(.type=="Running") | .message' 2>/dev/null || echo "N/A")
    
    echo "$(date): Status=$STATUS, Current Node=$CURRENT_NODE"
    
    sleep 2
done

echo "Migration completed!"
EOF

chmod +x migration-monitor.sh
Run the monitoring script during migration:
./migration-monitor.sh
